# 1. Understanding Asymptotic Notation

## Big O Notation and Algorithm Analysis

Big O notation is a mathematical framework used to describe the upper bound of an algorithm's running time or space requirements in terms of the input size ($n$). It helps developers evaluate how an application scales by analyzing efficiency trends, allowing engineers to identify and eliminate potential performance bottlenecks before code goes live into production.

## Search Operation Scenarios

1. Best-Case Scenario
   * The execution configuration where the algorithm performs the minimum number of steps.
   * For search routines, this occurs when the targeted element is the very first item inspected ($O(1)$).

2. Average-Case Scenario
   * The expected execution performance evaluated over a random distribution of inputs.
   * For search algorithms, this represents finding the item somewhere around the middle of the collection.

3. Worst-Case Scenario
   * The maximum bound of execution steps an algorithm can possibly perform.
   * This happens when the targeted item is at the absolute end of the array or is completely missing from the collection ($O(n)$ for linear structures).

